{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000102",
  "title": "Julia Sets",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nplotters = \"0.3\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Julia Sets \ud83c\udfa8\n\n**Julia sets** are close relatives of the Mandelbrot set. While the Mandelbrot iterates z = z\u00b2 + c with z\u2080 = 0 and *varies c* across the image, a Julia set *fixes c* and varies the starting point z\u2080.\n\nEach point *c* in the complex plane defines a unique Julia set. Points inside the Mandelbrot set produce *connected* Julia sets; points outside produce disconnected \"Fatou dust.\"\n\n## The Connection\n\nThe Mandelbrot set is essentially a *map* of all Julia sets \u2014 each pixel of the Mandelbrot tells you what the Julia set at that *c* value looks like:\n- **Inside the Mandelbrot** \u2192 connected Julia set\n- **On the boundary** \u2192 maximally intricate Julia set\n- **Outside the Mandelbrot** \u2192 disconnected dust",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Julia Set: c = \u22120.7 + 0.27i",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\n// Julia set constant: c = -0.7 + 0.27015i\n// This produces a beautiful dendritic (tree-like) fractal\nlet (c_re, c_im) = (-0.7_f64, 0.27015);\n\nlet (width, height) = (800u32, 600u32);\nlet (cols, rows) = (200u32, 150u32);\nlet max_iter = 150u32;\n\n// Symmetric view centered on the origin\nlet (x_min, x_max) = (-1.5_f64, 1.5);\nlet (y_min, y_max) = (-1.125_f64, 1.125);\n\nfn color_map(n: u32, max_n: u32) -> RGBColor {\n    if n == max_n {\n        return RGBColor(0, 0, 0);\n    }\n    let t = n as f64 / max_n as f64;\n    RGBColor(\n        (9.0 * (1.0 - t) * t * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (15.0 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (8.5 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * 255.0) as u8,\n    )\n}\n\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (width, height))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&BLACK).unwrap();\n\n    let cell_w = width / cols;\n    let cell_h = height / rows;\n\n    for row in 0..rows {\n        for col in 0..cols {\n            // For Julia sets, z\u2080 is the pixel coordinate\n            let mut zx = x_min + (col as f64 + 0.5) / cols as f64 * (x_max - x_min);\n            let mut zy = y_min + (row as f64 + 0.5) / rows as f64 * (y_max - y_min);\n\n            // Iterate z = z\u00b2 + c with fixed c\n            let mut n = 0u32;\n            while zx * zx + zy * zy <= 4.0 && n < max_iter {\n                let tmp = zx * zx - zy * zy + c_re;\n                zy = 2.0 * zx * zy + c_im;\n                zx = tmp;\n                n += 1;\n            }\n\n            let x0 = (col * cell_w) as i32;\n            let y0 = (row * cell_h) as i32;\n            root.draw(&Rectangle::new(\n                [(x0, y0), (x0 + cell_w as i32, y0 + cell_h as i32)],\n                color_map(n, max_iter).filled(),\n            ))\n            .unwrap();\n        }\n    }\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_1\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Julia Set: c = 0.285 + 0.01i",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\n// A different Julia constant: c = 0.285 + 0.01i\n// This creates an elegant spiral galaxy pattern\nlet (c_re, c_im) = (0.285_f64, 0.01);\n\nlet (width, height) = (800u32, 600u32);\nlet (cols, rows) = (200u32, 150u32);\nlet max_iter = 150u32;\n\nlet (x_min, x_max) = (-1.5_f64, 1.5);\nlet (y_min, y_max) = (-1.125_f64, 1.125);\n\nfn color_map(n: u32, max_n: u32) -> RGBColor {\n    if n == max_n {\n        return RGBColor(0, 0, 0);\n    }\n    let t = n as f64 / max_n as f64;\n    // Shifted palette for visual variety: purple \u2192 blue \u2192 cyan\n    RGBColor(\n        (8.5 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (9.0 * (1.0 - t) * t * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n        (15.0 * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * t * t * 255.0) as u8,\n    )\n}\n\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (width, height))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&BLACK).unwrap();\n\n    let cell_w = width / cols;\n    let cell_h = height / rows;\n\n    for row in 0..rows {\n        for col in 0..cols {\n            let mut zx = x_min + (col as f64 + 0.5) / cols as f64 * (x_max - x_min);\n            let mut zy = y_min + (row as f64 + 0.5) / rows as f64 * (y_max - y_min);\n\n            let mut n = 0u32;\n            while zx * zx + zy * zy <= 4.0 && n < max_iter {\n                let tmp = zx * zx - zy * zy + c_re;\n                zy = 2.0 * zx * zy + c_im;\n                zx = tmp;\n                n += 1;\n            }\n\n            let x0 = (col * cell_w) as i32;\n            let y0 = (row * cell_h) as i32;\n            root.draw(&Rectangle::new(\n                [(x0, y0), (x0 + cell_w as i32, y0 + cell_h as i32)],\n                color_map(n, max_iter).filled(),\n            ))\n            .unwrap();\n        }\n    }\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_2\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "How c Changes the Shape",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## \ud83c\udfad How *c* Changes the Shape\n\nThe parameter *c* completely determines the Julia set's geometry:\n\n| c value | Character |\n|---------|-----------|\n| c = 0 | Perfect circle (unit disk) |\n| c = \u22121 | \"Basilica\" \u2014 two tangent circles |\n| c = \u22120.123 + 0.745i | \"Douady's rabbit\" \u2014 three lobes |\n| c = i | Dendrite \u2014 tree-like, no interior |\n| c = \u22122 | Line segment \\[\u22122, 2\\] |\n\nThe boundary between connected and disconnected Julia sets traces out exactly the Mandelbrot set! Try picking a *c* value from the Mandelbrot notebook and rendering its Julia set here.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ]
}
